THIRD-PARTY DATASHEET TEXT INSIDE THE ARCHIVE
  The archived traces in archive/*.jsonl record an agent navigating technical
  datasheets published by third parties. Two kinds of field quote those
  documents: `tool_output_summary` (what a tool returned) and `agent_reasoning`
  (the model's own working, which frequently restates what it read).

    - Si115x                Silicon Laboratories Inc.
    - ACS70331, A4988       Allegro MicroSystems, LLC
    - DPS310                Infineon Technologies AG (ours, but see below)

  We would rather state the extent plainly than characterise it favourably.
  Measured as verbatim six-word-sequence overlap between each document's text
  layer and the whole archive:

    Si115x    ~35%      ACS70331  ~25%
    DPS310    ~27%      A4988     <1%

  Individual `tool_output_summary` records are capped at about 1,500
  characters, and `agent_reasoning` is not capped. The longest contiguous
  verbatim run we measured is roughly 270 words. Some individual pages are
  covered far more heavily than the document average, and complete parametric
  tables (for example the Si115x recommended-operating-conditions table) appear
  in full.

  What is NOT present: any figure, diagram or image; any page layout; and any
  ordering that would let a document be reassembled -- the material is
  scattered across thousands of records in the order an agent happened to read
  it, interleaved with model output.

  This text is reproduced for one purpose: so that published evaluation
  results can be independently verified. That is a transformative,
  non-commercial research use, and the underlying datasheets are deliberately
  not included in this repository. The text nevertheless remains the copyright
  of the respective manufacturers and is NOT covered by the MIT grant above.
  No permission to redistribute the underlying datasheets is granted or
  implied.

  The DPS310 is Infineon's own document, so its copyright sits with the same
